Using RPMB
==========

To use RPMB, we need an "Authentication Key". This key is programmed
into a one-time programmable region of the storage device. Subsequent
write operations to the RPMB check the authenticity of the incoming
message using this key. If key verification fails, the operation fails
too.

There are 2 ways to program an Authentication Key. The first is to
generate it using the CFG_RPMB_WRITE_KEY=y flag while compiling
optee_os. The second is to use other tools, such as ``mmc-tools``, to
write a key into the eMMC.

In case of the first option, optee_os generates a key and writes it
into the eMMC if it detects that a key isn't written already. To do
so, it sends the key in plaintext to the normal world. 
